WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
State.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:06.035
What the agent knows about its situation decides everything it can learn.

3
00:00:06.035 --> 00:00:07.833
Getting that right is your job.

4
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:11.767
A state is everything the agent knows right now.

5
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:17.167
The key assumption is the Markov property: given the present, the past adds nothing.

6
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:22.667
Real problems rarely obey it, so you must build states that make it approximately true.

7
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:25.867
Imagine a probe drifting through space.

8
00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:29.333
Its position is x equals four, y equals two.

9
00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:31.667
It might be drifting up and to the right.

10
00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:33.475
Or down and to the left.

11
00:00:33.475 --> 00:00:35.549
Same position, different futures.

12
00:00:35.549 --> 00:00:39.633
Add velocity, and the present carries what the past mattered for.

13
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:42.800
The classic fix is frame stacking.

14
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.467
DQN on Atari takes four consecutive frames.

15
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.400
It stacks them into one state.

16
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.033
Now the network sees which way things move.

17
00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:53.433
Here it is in code.

18
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.666
This state holds only position.

19
00:00:55.666 --> 00:00:57.100
Many futures follow it.

20
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.067
Adding velocity fixes that.

21
00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:02.067
Now the next position follows from the state alone.

22
00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:06.233
Say you train an agent to manage a satellite battery.

23
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.888
Charge percent alone is not Markov.

24
00:01:08.888 --> 00:01:13.933
Sixty percent charging and sixty percent discharging need opposite actions.

25
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:17.767
Add charge rate and a sunlight flag from the telemetry stream.

26
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.710
The agent stops thrashing.

27
00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.100
State design fixed it.

28
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.120
One more trap.

29
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.167
Sometimes the sensor cannot see something important.

30
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:29.237
That makes it a POMDP, partially observable.

31
00:01:29.237 --> 00:01:33.267
The agent needs memory, like a recurrent network or transformer.

32
00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:35.198
Let's recap.

33
00:01:35.198 --> 00:01:38.000
State is everything the agent knows right now.

34
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.700
Markov means the present alone predicts what comes next.

35
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.567
Add velocity, or stack frames, to get there.

36
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:48.656
And hidden information makes it a POMDP, which needs memory.

37
00:01:48.656 --> 00:01:49.667
Next up: action.
